{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775377289-NBbPi7u2IkfneB4B2Q47","role":"assistant","created":1775377305,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Hmm, the task asks for High-grade corporate bond yields for January to June 1938, but based on my search:\n\n1. The Treasury Bulletin from February 1947 (which is what we have) shows a table with monthly corporate bond yields\n2. Looking at the data, it shows yields for:\n   - 1936 (January to December)\n   - Then jumps to 1939 \n   - Then 1940, 1941, 1942, etc.\n3. There's NO 1938 data in this table\n\nBut wait - I saw a footnote that says \"Monthly figures for the period January 1937 through June 1938 are not presently available\" - but that was in a different context. Let me re-read that footnote more carefully in context of the bond yields.\n\nActually, wait - I should check again. Looking at the page more carefully, maybe I missed the 1938 data in the table. The table shows:\n- 1936: Jan-Dec \n- 1939: Jan-Dec\n- Then other years\n\nBut wait - the table could have a different structure. Let me look at the table more carefully. The first row is \"1936-Jan\" with values 2.69, 3.17 for Treasury bonds and the third value is for high-grade corporate bonds. But wait - I need to check what the third column value is for.\n\nActually, looking at the header:\n- Date\n- Partially tax-exempt Treasury bonds \n- Taxable Treasury bonds\n- High-grade corporate bonds\n- Date\n- Partially tax-exempt Treasury bonds\n- Taxable Treasury bonds\n- High-grade corporate bonds\n- Date\n- Partially tax-exempt Treasury bonds\n- Taxable Treasury bonds\n- High-grade corporate bonds\n\nSo the table has 3 sets of columns side by side. The first row shows:\n- \"1936-Jan.\" with values 2.69, 3.17 (this would be Treasury bonds - first set)\n- \"1941-Jan.\" with values 2.12, 2.57 (Treasury bonds - second set)\n\n\n- \"1944-Jan.\" with values 1.95, 2.49, 2.65 (Treasury bonds + corporate - third set)\n\nI'm noticing a pattern in the table structure. Each column set seems to represent different years, with Treasury bond yields in the first two columns and corporate bond yields in the third column. The first set covers 1936, the second set covers 1941, and the third set covers 1944. However, I'm still puzzled about the missing 1938 data.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_28fa86f0ab31b3a6","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\n# Let me properly parse the monthly table - extract the yields correctly\nimport re\n\nwith open('/app/resources/treasury_bulletin_1947_02_page_87.txt', 'r') as f:\n    content = f.read()\n\n# Find monthly series section\nmatch = re.search(r'(Monthly series.*?)Weekly series', content, re.DOTALL)\nif match:\n    section = match.group(1)\n    \n    # The table structure is complex - let me extract using better parsing\n    # The table has rows like <tr><td>year-month</td><td>val</td><td>val</td>...\n    # But formatted in a special way with 3 column groups\n    \n    # Let me use a different approach - split by rows and parse\n    table_section = section.replace('<table>', '').replace('</table>', '')\n    rows = re.findall(r'<tr><td>(.*?)</td></tr>', table_section)\n    \n    print(\"Rows found:\", len(rows))\n    for row in rows[:5]:\n        print(repr(row[:200]))\n        print(\"---\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
